 down  to understand how the data was generated  what the analysis was and how was that  interpreted and used  by the authors and we have several other  examples  including several different projects on  immunology oncology  biotechnology  and even agriculture so we do have  several different examples that you can  work through and those are called  projects so you will find there on the  portal how you can go between courses  and projects and the courses kind of  talk about the methods and then the  projects introduce you to what is the  research question what is the data set  and how would you approach  addressing or answering that question  with  data analysis methods  so to summarize  these kinds of methods that we will  learn and the experience that you will  get from these courses are is going to  prepare you to not only  read papers and understand how these  visuals that you see in papers were  produced  but also technically to understand how  to connect the different steps of  analysis with the intended outcome so  when you have that figure in mind or you  have that question in mind what are the  steps to go from raw data to process  data to hypothesis testing or to other  methods of analysis and annotation  we will also talk about how to try and  apply the same methods to novel data  sets and here you can either find  many different rna-seq or transcriptomic  data sets online  on public repositories like ncbi  or how to apply it to a data set of your  own so again for those of you that are  from lsu and lbrn if you are  participating in the lbrm research  program or the lsu if you're working in  some kind of lab and you have some of  that data you will be able to upload  that data to the platform and start  analyzing it or if you already have  process data you will learn how to  analyze the downstream part of the  analysis either on your computer  or  we will show you some other environments  where you can do it online or on the  cloud  so to kind of see  s